### Is there an existing issue for this?

I searched open and closed issues and did not find this reported.

### Describe the bug

ASP.NET Core **10.0.9** added an authenticated-user check to `DefaultPolicy.ServeResponseAsync`:

```csharp
if (context.HttpContext.User?.Identity?.IsAuthenticated == true)
{
    context.AllowCacheStorage = false;
    return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
}
```

Until 10.0.8 that check existed only in `DefaultPolicy.CacheRequestAsync`. A custom `IOutputCachePolicy` could therefore opt an authenticated response back into caching by setting `AllowCacheStorage = true` in its own `CacheRequestAsync`, because policies run in registration order and the custom one runs after the default. This is the documented way to cache responses that do not vary by caller, and the [output caching docs](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/performance/caching/output) describe overriding the default policy's opinions as the intended extension point.

From 10.0.9, that override is undone at the end of the request unless the custom policy *also* re-asserts `AllowCacheStorage = true` in its `ServeResponseAsync`. A policy that only ever *clears* the flag there — a natural shape, since the default policy's own `ServeResponseAsync` only clears it — now caches nothing at all.

Two things make this hard to notice:

1. **It fails silently.** No log, no exception, no header. Responses stay correct; they are simply never stored. In our case an image service ran with its entire output cache dead across every environment for weeks before anyone looked.
2. **Policy unit tests do not catch it.** A test that hands `ServeResponseAsync` a context where `AllowCacheStorage` is already `true` still passes, because the policy never sets it to `false`. Only a test starting from `false` — the real state after the default policy has run — fails.

I could not find this behaviour change in the [10.0.9 release notes](https://github.com/dotnet/core/blob/main/release-notes/10.0/10.0.9/10.0.9.md); the three CVEs in that release are unrelated. If it is intentional hardening it seems worth documenting as a breaking change, since it silently disables caching for existing, previously working policies.

### Expected Behavior

Either of:

- A custom policy's `AllowCacheStorage = true` set in `CacheRequestAsync` continues to hold through `ServeResponseAsync`, as it did up to 10.0.8; or
- the change is documented as breaking, so that authors of custom policies know they must now re-assert the flag in `ServeResponseAsync`.

### Steps To Reproduce

Minimal app — no authentication handler needed, only an authenticated `ClaimsPrincipal`:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.OutputCaching;
using System.Security.Claims;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddOutputCache(options =>
    options.AddPolicy("always", policy => policy.AddPolicy<AlwaysCachePolicy>()));

var app = builder.Build();

// Stand-in for any authentication scheme: the request is authenticated.
app.Use(async (context, next) =>
{
    context.User = new ClaimsPrincipal(new ClaimsIdentity(authenticationType: "test"));
    await next(context);
});

app.UseOutputCache();
app.MapGet("/", () => DateTime.UtcNow.ToString("O")).CacheOutput("always");
app.Run();

// Opts an authenticated response back into caching, overriding DefaultPolicy.
internal sealed class AlwaysCachePolicy : IOutputCachePolicy
{
    public ValueTask CacheRequestAsync(OutputCacheContext context, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        context.EnableOutputCaching = true;
        context.AllowCacheLookup = true;
        context.AllowCacheStorage = true;
        context.AllowLocking = true;
        context.ResponseExpirationTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5);
        context.CacheVaryByRules.QueryKeys = "";
        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }

    public ValueTask ServeFromCacheAsync(OutputCacheContext context, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        => ValueTask.CompletedTask;

    public ValueTask ServeResponseAsync(OutputCacheContext context, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
        => ValueTask.CompletedTask;
}
```

`curl` the root twice, a couple of seconds apart, and compare the bodies.

**10.0.8**

```
request 1 body: 2026-09-18T12:06:35.3126148Z
request 2 body: 2026-09-18T12:06:35.3126148Z
Age header    : Age: 2
=> cached
```

**10.0.9**

```
request 1 body: 2026-09-18T12:06:38.4698720Z
request 2 body: 2026-09-18T12:06:40.4984897Z
Age header    : <absent>
=> not cached
```

Adding `context.AllowCacheStorage = true;` to `AlwaysCachePolicy.ServeResponseAsync` restores caching on 10.0.9.

### Exceptions (if any)

None. The failure is silent.

### .NET Version

Broken from 10.0.9; confirmed still present in 10.0.12. Last good is 10.0.8.

### Anything else?

Isolated by swapping a single assembly between two otherwise untouched shared frameworks, in both directions, against an unchanged application:

| framework | `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OutputCaching.dll` | result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 10.0.8 | 10.0.8 | cached |
| 10.0.8 | **10.0.9** | not cached |
| 10.0.9 | **10.0.8** | cached |
| 10.0.9 | 10.0.9 | not cached |

Bisected across 10.0.0, 10.0.6, 10.0.8, 10.0.9 and 10.0.12. The source diff came from comparing the two assemblies' informational-version commits (`94ea82652cdd4e0f8046b5bd5becbd11461482ca` → `901ca941248413c79832d2fdbd709da0c4386353`) in the `dotnet/dotnet` VMR, where the only `OutputCaching` files touched are `src/aspnetcore/src/Middleware/OutputCaching/src/Policies/DefaultPolicy.cs` and its test file.

## Background and Motivation

The Identity UI `Manage/SetPassword` page adds a password to an account that does not have one, which
is how someone who signed up with an external login gets a password. Right now the only thing it checks
is the sign-in cookie.

I want the page to ask the user to confirm with a credential the account already has before it accepts a
new password, the same gate the Blazor template got in #68522, reauthentication before adding a passkey
or setting a password. The views need somewhere to read the confirmation state from and two handlers to
post to, and in Identity UI that means the page model.

## Proposed API

Identical additions in the V4 and V5 namespaces, since Identity UI ships both.

```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.UI.V5.Pages.Account.Manage.Internal;

public abstract class SetPasswordModel : PageModel
{
    public SetPasswordModel.InputModel Input { get; set; }
    public string? StatusMessage { get; set; }
+   public bool IsReauthenticated { get; set; }
+   public IList<UserLoginInfo>? CurrentLogins { get; set; }

    public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnGetAsync();
    public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnPostAsync();
+   public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnPostReauthenticateAsync(string provider);
+   public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnGetReauthenticationCallbackAsync();
}
```

```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.UI.V4.Pages.Account.Manage.Internal;

public abstract class SetPasswordModel : PageModel
{
    public SetPasswordModel.InputModel Input { get; set; }
    public string? StatusMessage { get; set; }
+   public bool IsReauthenticated { get; set; }
+   public IList<UserLoginInfo>? CurrentLogins { get; set; }

    public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnGetAsync();
    public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnPostAsync();
+   public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnPostReauthenticateAsync(string provider);
+   public virtual Task<IActionResult> OnGetReauthenticationCallbackAsync();
}
```

`CurrentLogins` is nullable to separate "this store cannot support the feature" from "this account has no
linked logins". The two need different messages: the first is something the app developer has to fix, the
second is something the user can fix.

## Usage Examples

Nobody calls these from C#. They are reached from the view, which is the call site reviewers should judge:

```cshtml
@if (!Model.IsReauthenticated)
{
    @if (Model.CurrentLogins is null)
    {
        <p class="text-danger">Setting a password requires a user store that supports security stamps.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <form asp-page-handler="Reauthenticate" method="post">
            @foreach (var login in Model.CurrentLogins)
            {
                <button type="submit" name="provider" value="@login.LoginProvider">
                    Confirm with @login.ProviderDisplayName
                </button>
            }
        </form>
    }
}
```

`OnGetReauthenticationCallbackAsync` is the return leg of the provider challenge, reached as
`?handler=ReauthenticationCallback`.

## Risks

**Behaviour change on an existing path: yes, two of them.** A user store that does not support security
stamps can no longer set a password through this page, because the confirmation is bound to the stamp and
there is nothing to bind to. The page says so explicitly rather than failing as a refused confirmation.
Separately, an account whose linked provider is no longer configured in the app has nothing to confirm
with and cannot set a password here.

